---

<DocsLayout title="Sessions">
  <h1>Sessions</h1>
  <p>A "session" in Identity is a refresh-token node in EntDB. There's no server-side session store other than the rotating refresh tokens; access tokens are stateless JWTs.</p>

  <h2>Session lifecycle</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Created</strong> on successful login (password, OAuth, passkey, TOTP, QR), invitation acceptance, or signup. The server creates a <code>RefreshToken</code> node (type_id 5) carrying the SHA-256 hash of the issued refresh token and metadata.</li>
    <li><strong>Used</strong> via the <code>RefreshToken</code> RPC. The old node is deleted and a fresh refresh token is issued, atomically.</li>
    <li><strong>Revoked</strong> via <code>Logout</code>, <code>RevokeSession</code>, <code>RevokeAllSessions</code>, or <code>SignOutEverywhere</code>. The node is deleted; subsequent refresh attempts with that token fail.</li>
    <li><strong>Expired</strong> automatically when the stored expiry timestamp passes. Default lifetime: 7 days.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Tracked metadata</h2>
  <p>Each session carries the data shown in the proto <code>Session</code> message:</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Field</th><th>Source</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>session_id</code></td><td>The refresh-token node ID (not the raw token)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>device_name</code></td><td>Best-effort, derived from <code>User-Agent</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>ip_address</code></td><td>Client IP at session creation</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>user_agent</code></td><td>Raw User-Agent header</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>created_at</code></td><td>When the session started</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>last_used_at</code></td><td>Updated on each refresh</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>expires_at</code></td><td>Created-at + <code>GATEWAY_REFRESH_EXPIRY_SECONDS</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>current</code></td><td>True for the session belonging to the calling token</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2>Listing sessions</h2>
  <pre is:raw><code class="language-bash">curl -s -X POST http://localhost/identity.IdentityService/ListMySessions \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $ACCESS" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{}'</code></pre>
  <p>Returns every active <code>RefreshToken</code> node belonging to the caller's user.</p>

  <h2>Revoking a single session</h2>
  <pre is:raw><code class="language-bash">curl -s -X POST http://localhost/identity.IdentityService/RevokeSession \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $ACCESS" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"session_id":"node_01HX..."}'</code></pre>
  <p>This deletes the refresh-token node. The corresponding access token (if any) keeps working until it expires &mdash; that's the price of stateless JWT verification.</p>

  <Callout type="warning" title="Access tokens outlive revocation">
    Because access tokens aren't checked against a revocation list, an access token issued just before revocation is valid for up to <code>GATEWAY_JWT_EXPIRY_SECONDS</code> (default 15 min) more. Keep that lifetime short and rely on refresh-token rotation for security &mdash; not access-token revocation.
  </Callout>

  <h2>Revoke all sessions</h2>
  <p>Two RPCs do this, both of which require the user's password as confirmation:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>RevokeAllSessions</code> &mdash; revokes every session except the current one.</li>
    <li><code>SignOutEverywhere</code> &mdash; revokes every session including the current one. The caller must sign in again.</li>
  </ul>
  <pre is:raw><code class="language-bash">curl -s -X POST http://localhost/identity.IdentityService/SignOutEverywhere \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $ACCESS" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"password":"Sup3rStrong!Pass"}'
# {"revoked_count":4}</code></pre>

  <h2>Logout</h2>
  <p>Sending the current refresh token to <code>Logout</code> revokes only that session:</p>
  <pre is:raw><code class="language-bash">curl -s -X POST http://localhost/identity.IdentityService/Logout \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"refresh_token":"rt_..."}'</code></pre>
  <p><code>Logout</code> is auth-exempt &mdash; you can call it without a valid access token, which is useful when the client's access token has already expired.</p>

  <h2>QR login as a session source</h2>
  <p>QR cross-device login produces a session on the <em>new</em> device once an authenticated device approves it. The session metadata captures the new device's user agent and IP, not the approving device's.</p>
</DocsLayout>
